s of obesity and hyperglycaemia (with no described purple striae or moon facies) sufficient to support adrenal hyperfunction (Cushing syndrome) as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": "The absence of hallmark Cushing syndrome cutaneous features (striae) reduces support for adrenal hyperfunction (B3), though generalized obesity can occur in other glucocorticoid-related conditions."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the established history of obesity and progressive hyperglycaemia over the prior week suggest metabolic syndrome-induced systemic glucotoxicity as the central pathogenic mechanism?", "summary": "The gradual onset of hyperglycaemia in a patient with obesity aligns with metabolic syndrome's potential to cause systemic glucotoxicity (B4), though LR+ is low and cannot override more specific findings."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the painful, multisite erythematous rash inconsistent with typical dermatologic findings in diabetic hyperglycaemic crisis, which usually lacks cutaneous inflammation or necrolytic features?", "summary": "Diabetic hyperglycaemic crisis typically lacks inflammatory or painful rashes, making this finding less consistent with B1, though still not conclusive in exclusion."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a painful rash contradict the characteristic necrolytic migratory erythema typically associated with glucagonoma syndrome, reducing the likelihood of B2 (alpha cell tumor)?", "summary": "The presence of a painful, erythematous rash is inconsistent with glucagonoma syndrome's hallmark necrolytic migratory erythema, moderating against B2 despite subacute symptom alignment."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of classic Cushing syndrome cutaneous signs (e.g., striae) in the physical exam stronger evidence against B3 than against other glucagonoma or diabetic etiologies?", "summary": "Classic striae absence in physical exam significantly reduces support for adrenal hyperfunction (B3), but does not substantially exclude glucagonoma or other glucocorticoid-related mechanisms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the presence of a painful, erythematous rash inconsistent with metabolic syndrome as the sole explanatory framework for the patient’s condition?", "summary": "Metabolic syndrome alone does not cause painful erythematous rashes, moderately reducing its plausibility as the sole driver of systemic symptoms."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.562], ["B1.1", 0.307], ["B4", 0.234]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers (airway/respiratory compromise, shock, major haemodynamic instability, or herniation risk meningitis) or time-critical syndrome patterns requiring action before diagnostic expansion.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "B1 posterior (0.56) remains below 0.75 threshold, and B2 (alpha cell tumor) as dangerous alternative with 0.117 posterior and 0.7 danger requires further refinement. Info-exhaustion criteria not met (0.75 estimated remaining value)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.75, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.30681818181818177, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Chronic metabolic acidosis', 'Erythematous rash'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.562}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Diabetic Ketoacidosis (Type 1/T2-related)", "posterior": 0.307, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B4.1", "B2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=29%, source=both]\n\nDiabetic Ketoacidosis (Type 1/T2-related) vs Insulin Resistance (Pathological Contribution to Glucotoxicity):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, autosomal recessive inheritance, beta-cell dysfunction, confusion\n  Favours second: abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n\nRelated to Diabetic Ketoacidosis (Type 1/T2-related): inborn errors of metabolism, type 2 diabetes mellitus\n\nRelated to Insulin Resistance (Pathological Contribution to Glucotoxicity): diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.55):]\n  Source: A glucagon-secreting alpha-cell carcinoma of the pancreas. > Introduction\n  Glucagonomas are rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (pNETs) that originate from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans. While most cases are sporadic, a subset may be associated with multiple \n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: A glucagon-secreting alpha-cell carcinoma of the pancreas. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Glucagonoma is a rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor (pNET) originating from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans, which produce glucagon. Most cases are sporadic, though about 20% are associa\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: A glucagon-secreting alpha-cell carcinoma of the pancreas. > Introduction\n  Glucagonomas are rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (pNETs) that originate from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans. While most cases are sporadic, a subset may be associated with multiple \n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: A glucagon-secreting alpha-cell carcinoma of the pancreas. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Glucagonoma is a rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor (pNET) originating from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans, which produce glucagon. Most cases are sporadic, though about 20% are associa\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  painful rash → necrolytic migrating erythema → Endocrine Neoplasm with Glucagon Secretion / Alpha Cell Tumor\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Obtain biopsy of the rash to confirm necrolytic migrating erythema, which would strongly support glucagonoma diagnosis\n  abdominal pain and diarrhea → insulin resistance exacerbation → Progressive Beta-Cell Dysfunction in Context of Metabolic Syndrome\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for evidence of progressive insulin resistance (e.g., elevated HOMA-IR) in the context of abdominal symptoms and metabolic syndrome\n  medical history of obesity → insulin resistance syndrome → Insulin Resistance (Pathological Contribution to Glucotoxicity)\n    (frequency: obligate)\n    Suggestion: Correlate obesity with quantitative measures of hepatic/fatty acid dysfunction to characterize insulin resistance severity\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
